WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.952
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.952 --> 00:00:02.652
Wednesday the eighteenth.

00:00:02.652 --> 00:00:07.752
A bright, mild one, with a full calendar that keeps going into the evening.

00:00:07.752 --> 00:00:10.268
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:10.268 --> 00:00:15.300
Past about what aperture do most lenses start going soft from diffraction?

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.660
Answer's at the end.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.904
Weather's nothing to plan around.

00:00:18.904 --> 00:00:26.248
Sunny and dry, mid-forties this morning, up to the low sixties by early afternoon, with a light west breeze.

00:00:26.248 --> 00:00:29.716
Tomorrow looks about the same, just a colder start.

00:00:29.716 --> 00:00:30.668
Okay, the day.

00:00:30.668 --> 00:00:33.728
Calendar has the gym at seven, straight sets.

00:00:33.728 --> 00:00:35.632
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:35.632 --> 00:00:38.828
There's an appointment on there at five-thirty.

00:00:38.828 --> 00:00:44.472
The lens return is on there for six-thirty, though the time still isn't really set.

00:00:44.472 --> 00:00:46.444
And a shelf project at seven.

00:00:46.444 --> 00:00:52.564
So the evening's stacked, and grabbing food before five-thirty would make the rest easier.

00:00:52.564 --> 00:00:53.516
Alright, news.

00:00:53.516 --> 00:00:56.100
The Nvidia story got its next chapter.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.112
The company reported last night, and the numbers were huge.

00:01:00.112 --> 00:01:09.836
Revenue more than doubled from a year ago, to almost a hundred and sixteen billion dollars for the quarter, and it beat what everyone expected.

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:12.148
But the bigger news was the board.

00:01:12.148 --> 00:01:18.404
It said it won't bring Jensen Huang back as C.E.O., and for the first time it gave a reason.

00:01:18.404 --> 00:01:25.136
It pointed to personal behavior that didn't fit the company's code of conduct, without saying more.

00:01:25.136 --> 00:01:33.160
The finance chief stays in charge while they search for someone permanent, and the stock went up a little after hours.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:34.180
Closer to home.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:37.716
The Flatbush Avenue busway opened yesterday morning.

00:01:37.716 --> 00:01:43.972
It's a stretch of Flatbush set aside mostly for buses, so they stop crawling in car traffic.

00:01:43.972 --> 00:01:46.896
Streetsblog says it's already working well.

00:01:46.896 --> 00:01:57.300
The same day, the mayor and the governor cut the ribbon on a one point four billion dollar widening of the Van Wyck in Queens, which is the opposite bet.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.592
More room for cars.

00:01:58.592 --> 00:02:00.836
And in Ukraine, a big escalation.

00:02:00.836 --> 00:02:09.608
Ukraine hit the Kerch bridge, the one connecting Russia to Crimea, during what Russia called the largest drone attack of the war.

00:02:09.608 --> 00:02:13.960
Russia says it shot down more than two thousand drones in a day.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:19.536
Traffic on the bridge got restricted, and thousands of cars were reportedly stuck.

00:02:19.536 --> 00:02:26.064
Russia also hit Kyiv hard, killing at least seven people and cutting power to tens of thousands.

00:02:26.064 --> 00:02:29.872
The peace talks are still stuck on the same thing, land.

00:02:29.872 --> 00:02:31.912
Okay, here's one for Saturday.

00:02:31.912 --> 00:02:38.848
Starr Suites, a gallery on Starr Street in Bushwick, opens a new show this Saturday, the twenty-first.

00:02:38.848 --> 00:02:44.356
It's a painter named Cathy Diamond, and the show's called In Each Other's Orbits.

00:02:44.356 --> 00:02:51.972
It runs through the end of December, so there's no rush, but the first weekend is when a space feels most alive.

00:02:51.972 --> 00:02:56.256
It's free to walk in, and it's an easy stop on a slow Saturday.

00:02:56.256 --> 00:02:58.636
Today's challenge is for your head.

00:02:58.636 --> 00:03:02.648
Tonight, once you're in bed, replay the whole day backward.

00:03:02.648 --> 00:03:10.332
Start with lying down, then the shelf, the lens, the appointment, work, all the way back to the gym this morning.

00:03:10.332 --> 00:03:11.896
Take about two minutes.

00:03:11.896 --> 00:03:15.568
Don't judge any of it, just watch it go by in reverse.

00:03:15.568 --> 00:03:22.028
It's a weirdly good memory workout, and it tends to knock loose the one thing you forgot to do.

00:03:22.028 --> 00:03:24.544
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:24.544 --> 00:03:30.256
Remember Gemini only handing back raw P.C.M., and the memo turning it into an M.P.3?

00:03:30.256 --> 00:03:31.276
You know P.C.M.

00:03:31.276 --> 00:03:33.316
is every sample, written down.

00:03:33.316 --> 00:03:34.880
Today's the other side.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.892
What M.P.3 actually throws away, and why you never hear it.

00:03:38.892 --> 00:03:40.796
The trick is called masking.

00:03:40.796 --> 00:03:42.768
Your ears get swamped easily.

00:03:42.768 --> 00:03:49.228
A loud sound hides quieter sounds near it in pitch, and even for a split second right after it.

00:03:49.228 --> 00:03:54.464
So M.P.3 chops the audio into tiny slices, about a fortieth of a second each.

00:03:54.464 --> 00:04:01.332
For every slice, it works out what your ear can't possibly hear right then, and just doesn't save it.

00:04:01.332 --> 00:04:03.916
Bitrate is the budget for what's left.

00:04:03.916 --> 00:04:06.772
A lower bitrate means it has to toss more.

00:04:06.772 --> 00:04:11.328
Music needs a big budget, lots of instruments, lots of high detail.

00:04:11.328 --> 00:04:12.552
One voice is easy.

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:17.516
A single steady voice can sound clean at a fraction of what a song needs.

00:04:17.516 --> 00:04:20.100
Then there's constant versus variable.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.160
Constant spends the same budget every second.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:28.396
Variable saves on easy stretches, like silence, and spends more on busy ones.

00:04:28.396 --> 00:04:29.824
And here's the catch.

00:04:29.824 --> 00:04:31.320
M.P.3 only works once.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:38.868
Encode an M.P.3 into another M.P.3, and the second pass makes its own guesses on top of the first one's damage.

00:04:38.868 --> 00:04:41.792
Every round, it gets a little more smeared.

00:04:41.792 --> 00:04:45.668
That's why you always encode straight from the raw P.C.M.

00:04:45.668 --> 00:04:49.748
Start from the full version, and only throw stuff away once.

00:04:49.748 --> 00:04:56.004
So: calendar says gym at seven, Godinger till five, then five-thirty, six-thirty, and seven.

00:04:56.004 --> 00:04:58.180
Replay the day backward tonight.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:04.096
The answer: around f eleven, which is why you stack focus instead of stopping way down.

00:05:04.096 --> 00:05:06.000
Have a good Wednesday, Ziad.
